.TH GIT-TRACK 1 "2026-11-02" "gitrack" "Git Track Manual"
.SH NAME
git-track \- distributed tracker whose state merges with your code
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git track
.I command
[\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Git Track is a distributed issue/task/note tracker whose state lives in the
git working tree and merges as part of an ordinary \fBgit merge\fR. It installs
as \fBgit-track\fR on \fBPATH\fR and runs as the \fBgit track\fR subcommand.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
initialize a tracker in the current repository
.TP
.B config status ...
edit status workflows (see 'config status')
.TP
.B create [\-k kind] <title>
create a track, print its id
.TP
.B update <ref> [flags]
change a track's title/status/kind/description
.TP
.B get [\-\-json] <ref>[.path]
read a field, list, or object (e.g. <ref>.comments.<id>)
.TP
.B set <ref>.<key> <value>
write a free\-form field (not state/status)
.TP
.B show <ref>
display a track (ref: id, short id, or slug)
.TP
.B list [\-\-state s] [\-s status] [\-k kind]
list tracks
.TP
.B search <query>
find tracks by text in title/description/slug
.TP
.B close <ref> [\-\-status=s]
close a track: state=closed, status=s (default done)
.TP
.B reopen <ref>
reopen a track: state=open, status=reopened
.TP
.B comment <ref> [\-m msg] [\-\-reply\-to=<cid>]
add a Markdown comment
.TP
.B serve [\-\-addr 127.0.0.1:0]
live loopback preview server (random port)
.TP
.B fsck [\-\-write]
verify (or rebuild) the derived cache
.SH MODEL
Every command is a verb. A track has two axes: a fixed state (open|closed) changed only by create/close/reopen, and a customizable status workflow (built\-ins new/reopened/done plus user\-defined statuses in config/statuses/) moved by 'update \-s'. update flags: \-t title | \-s status | \-k kind | \-d description. See docs/FORMAT.md for the on\-disk format.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .gitrack/
The tracker tree in the working directory: one directory per track keyed by
UUID, config under \fB.gitrack/config/\fR, and a git-ignored derived cache
under \fB.gitrack/cache/\fR. See the on-disk format specification.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Mark Ferrell.
